WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
I

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:48.040
Africa.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:01:00.040
The world's greatest wilderness.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:13.040
The only place on Earth to see the full majesty of nature.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.040
There's so much more here than we ever imagined.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:25.040
I'm standing where the equator cuts right across the middle of the continent.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.040
To the north of me, there's an immense desert the size of the United States of America.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.040
To the west, a vast rainforest the size of India.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:48.040
And behind me for thousands of miles, the most fertile savannas in the world.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:58.040
From the roof of Africa to the deepest jungle.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:04.040
Rarely seen places and untold stories.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:26.040
There's nowhere in the world where wildlife puts on a greater show.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:42.040
This is the last place on Earth where you can come eye to eye with the greatest animals that walk our planet.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:54.040
This is Africa.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:03:15.040
Our journey starts in the far southwest, in the oldest and strangest corner of the continent.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:27.040
Here, the Thursteinland is covered with thousands upon thousands of circles.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.040
We still don't know their origins.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:48.040
Poisonous plants, foraging insects and even magnetism have all been suggested, but each ruled out.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:04:02.040
The circles don't move and their shape never varies. They're unchanging, much like this part of Africa itself.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:18.040
Ancient and arid, it almost never rains on this land, yet there is water here hidden away.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:37.040
But to survive here, life must use every trick in the book.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.040
Winter.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:46.040
Dawn temperatures can fall well below freezing.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:56.040
And that's a problem for this drongo. It's too cold for his normal prey, flying insects.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:07.040
But he has a plan. The drongo is the Kalahari's greatest trickster.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:11.040
And these are his victims.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:20.040
A family of meerkats, desert specialists.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:27.040
After warming up in the morning sun, the meerkats begin their search for breakfast.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.040
The drongo can now begin his tricks.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:39.040
But he must first win the confidence of his victims.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:45.040
He spots an eagle on the hunt and sounds a warning.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:56.040
One that sends the meerkats gratefully scurrying to safety.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:00.040
Danger over.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:06.040
And now he has their trust.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:13.040
He sounds another warning.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:20.040
But this time it's a false alarm.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:27.040
Thank you very much.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:37.040
The meerkats fell for it.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:52.040
This all seems too easy. He tries the same trick again.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:07:05.040
But the meerkats aren't stupid. They'll only fall for it once.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:12.040
The juicy scorpion won't be for him.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:17.040
Then suddenly the sound of a sentry's warning.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:22.040
No meerkat can ignore that. Sentries never lie.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:29.040
But the sentry sees no danger.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:39.040
Guess who?

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:47.040
Of course it's the drongo. He's learned to mimic the meerkats own warning call.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:53.040
And now he can enjoy his prize.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:08:03.040
A gang of meerkats outsmarted by a bird.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:13.040
The drongo is only deceitful in the hardest winter months. For the rest of the year he provides honest protection.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:26.040
So in the long run the meerkat family profit as well as the drongo.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:44.040
It's a much harder life if you haven't yet learned the tricks of your trade.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:09:13.040
This young leopard is just a year old and at a critical point in his life.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:25.040
His mother has battled to raise her two cubs. But finding enough food for them is now beyond her.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:38.040
From today he'll have to fend for himself.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:46.040
Lahari means land of great thirst.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:10:00.040
Prey is scarce. Of all the leopards in Africa these have to be the most resourceful.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:09.040
A big warthog. Potential prey. But armed and dangerous.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:31.040
His mother tried to tackle one but it nearly killed her.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:47.040
He spots something more promising.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:58.040
A steenbock. That's more like it.

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:12.040
He won't strike unless he can get to within just four meters and without making the slightest sound.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:28.040
A jackal barks an alarm. But the steenbock still has no idea it's being stalked.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:57.040
The nearer he gets, the quieter he must be.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:25.040
He's blown it.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:36.040
A good opportunity like that won't come around very often.

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:47.040
Hungry and thirsty he heads back home and spots a kill stashed in a tree, almost certainly by his own mother.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:13:03.040
And like any teenager he thinks nothing of raiding her larder.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:32.040
Booby trapped.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:52.040
It's not really his day, is it?

00:13:52.040 --> 00:14:10.040
Some young leopards grow up to be brilliant opportunists. But even they find life hard here in the Kalahari.

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:23.040
These bizarre little birds are baby ostriches. They're just a few days old.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:34.040
In time they'll become superb desert survivors. But in the Kalahari these early days are perilous.

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:42.040
Like leopards and meerkats, adult ostriches can extract all the moisture they require from their food.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:49.040
The chicks, however, won't survive much more than another day without water.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:15:11.040
But there's none in sight. How can their parents conjure up water out here?

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:22.040
The youngsters follow their parents as they head out onto a featureless wasteland.

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:38.040
It seems like a suicidal journey.

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:50.040
The Itosha salt pan. Here water is more often a mirage than reality.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:16:08.040
It's now well over 40 degrees centigrade. Their father shades his chicks from the midday sun.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:26.040
Another mirage? No. The ostrich family is not alone out here.

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:45.040
Surrounded by miles of sun-baked mud, sweet fresh water wells up from deep below ground. Like a miracle.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:17:04.040
Although the ostrich parents have guided their chicks to water, there's still a problem.

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:20.040
Traffic. Heavy traffic. These tiny fragile birds could easily be trampled underfoot.

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:49.040
The water is tantalizingly close. Where prey gathers, predators are never far behind.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:54.040
No damage!

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:23.200
The brawling lions have unwittingly done the young ostriches a favour.

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:26.200
The water hole is now clear.

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:32.680
Sometimes you need a bit of luck in life.

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:38.920
Sometimes you need a bit of luck in life.

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:56.360
Their first ever drink,

00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:10.840
Their first ever drink, and just in time.

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:28.920
Their father's done his job.

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.240
A black rhinoceros.

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:38.280
The Kalahari's most cantankerous resident.

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:44.960
They don't like company, and they certainly don't like sharing a water hole with lions.

00:19:44.960 --> 00:20:02.920
Fortunately, for everyone else that is, they only visit twice a week.

00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:10.840
The Kalahari is the black rhino's last stronghold.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:16.340
And here under the cover of darkness, at one secret and very special water hole, rhino

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:28.600
abandon their normally solitary life and come from miles around to meet under the stars.

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:35.440
Using the latest starlight camera, we can reveal for the first time the rhino's true

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:41.400
character.

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:44.600
This young female seems nervous.

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:48.200
She senses other rhinos close by.

00:20:48.200 --> 00:21:03.320
A mother appears from the shadows with her calf.

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:10.320
Tentatively they greet one another.

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:24.960
They may be ill-tempered by day, but now they become gentle and affectionate.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:35.780
More and more arrive.

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:54.400
We had no idea that rhinos met to socialize and build friendships like this.

00:21:54.400 --> 00:22:12.120
The young female has an admirer.

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:24.000
But she doesn't seem keen on him.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:28.440
She's excited about something.

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:35.280
Or someone.

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:43.200
Here comes a really big male.

00:22:43.200 --> 00:23:01.720
This time she's much more welcoming.

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:07.960
Who would have thought that rhino could be so flirtatious?

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:10.840
The first male tries to come between them.

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:21.000
Somehow or other he's got a pair of antelope horns stuck on his nose.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:28.440
It looks as if she's been won over by his eccentric style.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:51.800
He leads her off, away from the party.

00:23:51.800 --> 00:24:12.360
He may have style, but he's turning out to be something of a disappointment.

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:20.840
A girl can only put up with so much.

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:29.960
The only way she can get rid of him is to pretend she's asleep.

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:34.840
To see so many rhino in one place is a revelation.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:36.960
And that's the power water has here.

00:24:36.960 --> 00:25:02.680
The power to bring together the greatest gathering of rhinos anywhere on earth.

00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:08.080
The

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:15.840
towers above a plateau that is two billion years old.

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:24.880
This land has remained unchanged for longer than any other part of Africa.

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:30.520
Animals here have had a long time to find inventive solutions to the challenge of finding

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:57.680
water.

00:25:57.680 --> 00:26:06.520
Found on the open plains, life must await the chance arrival of rain.

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:13.720
When it does fall, it has an extraordinary effect.

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:29.280
Such sporadic downpour may only last minutes, but it can bring life, and in spectacular

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:32.280
numbers.

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:35.880
Redbill quia.

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:43.920
They're the most numerous bird in the world, in all more than a billion live here in the

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:45.240
Kalahai.

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:49.920
No one knows quite how, but they seem to have an extraordinary ability to locate the fall

00:26:49.920 --> 00:27:12.280
of rain and then instantly exploit the bonanza that follows.

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:29.760
These nomads now have just five weeks to find food, build a nest and raise a brood.

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:34.560
But they're not alone.

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:50.240
The rains have also created a plague.

00:27:50.240 --> 00:28:03.120
These are armored, round crickets, giant insects with voracious appetites for meat.

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:24.120
With the quilia parents away feeding, their chicks are defenseless.

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:46.840
Radios and ehuru can run away too, but are safe.

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:53.840
The adults return.

00:28:53.840 --> 00:29:10.520
But the cricket fights back,

00:29:10.520 --> 00:29:40.360
spreading its own foul-tasting blood into their eyes.

00:29:40.360 --> 00:29:55.920
The cricket is still alive, but the stench of its blood attracts the attention of others.

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:59.960
Now it is the target.

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:18.680
These crickets become cannibals.

00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:25.120
All too soon, the bonanza brought by the rain is over, and the quilia head off in search

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:45.560
of the next rare downpour.

00:30:45.560 --> 00:30:52.680
The Kalahari is scarred by rivers that have long since run dry, the water claimed by the

00:30:52.680 --> 00:31:06.600
thirsty land.

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:12.160
But it's not gone far.

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:34.800
Deep below lies a secret, one that was discovered only 25 years ago.

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:50.320
Humid air rushing to the surface gives this place its name, Dragon's Breath Cave.

00:31:50.320 --> 00:32:01.920
The shaft descends for 60 meters until it meets... water.

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:08.960
Here there is a massive chamber big enough to swallow three jumbo jets nose to tail,

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:12.840
filled with cool fresh water.

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:26.440
The world's largest underground lake.

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:31.120
This is fossil water.

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:42.680
It's been trapped here undisturbed for thousands, if not millions, of years.

00:32:42.680 --> 00:32:46.520
We have no idea how deep the lake is.

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:54.400
Divers have been down to 100 meters, and still there's no sign of the bottom.

00:32:54.400 --> 00:33:03.840
Remarkably, Dragon's Breath is part of a vast cave system that extends beneath the Kalahari

00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:07.480
for thousands of miles.

00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:24.200
Even here, in this lonely cave, there is life.

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:29.840
Golden catfish, only found in this one cave.

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:37.960
They're the rarest and most isolated fish in the world.

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:43.880
Life down here is as challenging as it is in the desert above.

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:53.360
There's no food except the debris that occasionally falls onto the surface.

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:56.960
And these catfish are totally blind.

00:33:56.960 --> 00:34:03.600
The only world they know is the one they sense through touch.

00:34:03.600 --> 00:34:12.640
A blind fish living in perpetual darkness, deep beneath one of the most arid regions

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:21.920
of Africa.

00:34:21.920 --> 00:34:24.200
Such cruel irony.

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:33.680
So much water hidden away out of reach.

00:34:33.680 --> 00:34:39.360
Along the western edge of the Kalahari, the land becomes so dry it seems impossible that

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:58.640
any life could survive here.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:00.040
The Namib.

00:35:00.040 --> 00:35:09.240
A million square miles of sand, exquisitely sculpted by the wind.

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:21.200
This is the oldest desert in the world.

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:28.600
Respite comes from fog rolling in from the Atlantic Ocean.

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:34.840
It condenses into a few precious drops.

00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:44.880
Just enough to sustain life.

00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:53.440
A pompilid wasp is searching the dunes.

00:35:53.440 --> 00:36:01.800
She's not looking for a drink, but for somewhere moist to lay her egg.

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:11.520
How will she pull off a trick like that?

00:36:11.520 --> 00:36:13.920
The entrance to a burrow.

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:22.800
That's worth investigating.

00:36:22.800 --> 00:36:28.280
She may be tiny, but once she decides to dig, she can shift extraordinary quantities of

00:36:28.280 --> 00:36:46.480
sand.

00:36:46.480 --> 00:36:55.200
She's unearthed this spider for a grisly purpose.

00:36:55.200 --> 00:37:01.000
It's so dry, the only place with enough moisture for her egg is within the body of another

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:04.080
living thing.

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:12.800
First, she must paralyze her victim.

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:33.640
And then the spider plays its trump card.

00:37:33.640 --> 00:37:52.560
The aptly named golden wheel spider can cartwheel fast enough to escape its gruesome fate.

00:37:52.560 --> 00:38:20.200
For the wasp, her near impossible search goes on.

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:25.880
If it's hard enough for a tiny wasp to survive here in the Namib, how is it possible for

00:38:25.880 --> 00:38:37.680
a giant?

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:42.160
A desert giraffe.

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:53.080
It's difficult to imagine how such a huge animal can live in a place with so little

00:38:53.080 --> 00:38:55.120
water.

00:38:55.120 --> 00:39:11.520
This old male is at the very limit of his endurance.

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:23.960
The land may be bone dry, but there are signs that water once flowed here.

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:28.800
The Ho'onib, one of Namibia's rivers.

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:46.080
A river of sand.

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:52.200
The trees that line these sand rivers send roots down over 30 meters to tap water that

00:39:52.200 --> 00:39:58.160
lies deep beneath the riverbed.

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:04.440
These trees are the giraffe's salvation, even if he has to stretch to his very tallest

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:12.160
to get a mouthful.

00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:31.600
Even on tiptoe, he still needs a half meter long tongue to reach the leaves he so badly

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:39.280
needs.

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:45.320
He's ruled the stretch of the Ho'onib for over a decade, and this prime territory is

00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:57.800
attracting females.

00:40:57.800 --> 00:41:10.600
He waits confidently for her.

00:41:10.600 --> 00:41:34.560
But they've got company.

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:56.840
The

00:41:56.840 --> 00:42:06.360
old bull won't tolerate a rival.

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:15.120
Pushing and shoving, they size each other up.

00:42:15.120 --> 00:42:32.040
The young rival seems to think he has a chance and attacks.

00:42:32.040 --> 00:42:46.240
The first few blows usually settle things in such battles, but here the stakes are high.

00:42:46.240 --> 00:42:53.080
To lose means exile in the desert.

00:42:53.080 --> 00:43:00.040
Neither will back down.

00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:03.840
As the fight intensifies, they change tactics.

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:07.640
The young male aims for the rump.

00:43:07.640 --> 00:43:34.040
The old bull targets his rival's legs.

00:43:34.040 --> 00:44:00.160
The

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:10.720
old bull is down.

00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:18.600
Is this the end of his reign?

00:44:18.600 --> 00:44:35.160
He knows a knockout blow is coming.

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:59.080
The old bull ducks and strikes a blow to his rival's underbelly.

00:44:59.080 --> 00:45:22.760
Out for the count.

00:45:29.080 --> 00:45:41.800
The old bull is victorious.

00:45:41.800 --> 00:45:52.080
But only just.

00:45:52.080 --> 00:46:07.600
The sand river remains his to rule.

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:27.480
It's a river that is about to be transformed.

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:33.760
Under clear blue skies, water floods down the Hoennib.

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:55.480
The welcome consequence of rain that fell hundreds of miles away.

00:46:55.480 --> 00:47:01.720
The water may only flow for a matter of hours.

00:47:01.720 --> 00:47:08.680
But this miraculous flood is enough to provide a lifeline for the trees and the giraffes

00:47:08.680 --> 00:47:16.840
of the Hoennib River.

00:47:16.840 --> 00:47:37.560
That's what makes this place worth fighting for.

00:47:37.560 --> 00:47:45.240
Here fossil lakes, secret water holes, desert fog and ephemeral rivers like this provide

00:47:45.240 --> 00:47:54.920
just enough water for life to get by, no matter how tough it gets.

00:47:54.920 --> 00:48:01.600
It's hard to find more inventive solutions to staying alive than in this, the most ancient

00:48:01.600 --> 00:48:27.960
corner of Africa.

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:33.680
For four years, the Africa team searched the continent for new and surprising stories,

00:48:33.680 --> 00:48:41.760
not only of strange and unfamiliar creatures, but also some we think we know.

00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:46.400
Veteran wildlife cameraman Martin Kulbeck took on the challenge of shedding new light

00:48:46.400 --> 00:48:54.960
on the life of Namibia's desert giraffe.

00:48:54.960 --> 00:48:58.360
I jumped at the opportunity of working with an animal that I hadn't really spent much

00:48:58.360 --> 00:48:59.360
time with.

00:48:59.360 --> 00:49:02.360
Straight away, they proved to be quite an eye-opener.

00:49:02.360 --> 00:49:05.520
They're very bizarre looking animals.

00:49:05.520 --> 00:49:12.920
We just kept looking at them from different angles and they looked even weirder.

00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:17.840
The combination of the sort of weird close-ups, the beautiful landscape that they're in, they're

00:49:17.840 --> 00:49:20.840
amusing.

00:49:20.840 --> 00:49:27.600
I got really attached to them actually.

00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:31.920
Overlying all this, we were always waiting for a fight.

00:49:31.920 --> 00:49:35.360
But to see a full-blooded fight is very rare.

00:49:35.360 --> 00:49:40.920
So, the only way that we were going to see it is if we stuck at it day after day, every

00:49:40.920 --> 00:49:44.340
day for 30 days.

00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:53.000
We were lucky enough that we found a male guarding a female.

00:49:53.000 --> 00:50:01.500
And out of nowhere, this male came around the corner.

00:50:01.500 --> 00:50:09.240
And almost immediately, faced up to our male.

00:50:09.240 --> 00:50:10.960
Absolutely no warning that this was going to happen.

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:14.400
So, it was complete pandemonium in the car.

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:19.120
But luckily, I got the camera up and running in time to actually capture this fight.

00:50:19.120 --> 00:50:27.680
And it all came down to one minute in real time.

00:50:27.680 --> 00:50:30.280
When I filmed it, you don't see it in slow motion.

00:50:30.280 --> 00:50:34.880
And you just have to go with the flow.

00:50:34.880 --> 00:50:36.920
You're not experiencing the fight.

00:50:36.920 --> 00:50:39.360
You're just basically framing it and capturing it.

00:50:39.360 --> 00:50:43.440
So, it was only afterwards when we looked at it in slow motion that you could really

00:50:43.440 --> 00:50:52.160
understand how ferocious it was.

00:50:52.160 --> 00:50:54.440
You can see the impacts on the skin.

00:50:54.440 --> 00:51:00.240
You can see the ripples going through the flesh.

00:51:00.240 --> 00:51:04.560
But it was the final blows that delivered the real surprise.

00:51:04.560 --> 00:51:10.680
It was like one of those chimneys falling down.

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:30.320
The last moment, the head just went plunk.

00:51:30.320 --> 00:51:34.920
And we thought it was dead.

00:51:34.920 --> 00:51:38.680
We thought this thing was dead.

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:42.280
And it lay there for, it must have been three minutes.

00:51:42.280 --> 00:51:45.800
Eventually, this thing suddenly got up.

00:51:45.800 --> 00:51:47.640
The one that was lying down.

00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:49.760
And the two of them were then standing.

00:51:49.760 --> 00:51:55.080
And then the one that had been knocked over completely then just said, I've had enough.

00:51:55.080 --> 00:52:03.160
Okay, okay, you won and I'm off.

00:52:03.160 --> 00:52:06.440
I think it's very unlikely I'm going to see anything like that again.

00:52:06.440 --> 00:52:08.360
I think that's a once in a lifetime.

00:52:08.360 --> 00:52:12.800
I really do.

00:52:12.800 --> 00:52:21.700
It won't be easy to look at giraffes in the same way again.

00:52:21.700 --> 00:52:27.200
On the other side of the desert, another of Africa's great animal icons was attracting

00:52:27.200 --> 00:52:37.120
the attention of the team as they staked out a secret water hole.

00:52:37.120 --> 00:52:45.800
They hope to reveal a very different side to the personality of the black rhinoceros.

00:52:45.800 --> 00:53:04.200
The team have heard that at night rhinos behave a little strangely.

00:53:04.200 --> 00:53:10.960
A specially built starlight camera would allow the team to pierce the darkness.

00:53:10.960 --> 00:53:17.480
It's amazing, that's filming something we can't even see.

00:53:17.480 --> 00:53:20.160
Yeah, we can look out there now.

00:53:20.160 --> 00:53:22.160
Yeah, it's just black, isn't it?

00:53:22.160 --> 00:53:25.720
But through this, it looks as sharp as day.

00:53:25.720 --> 00:53:27.960
Rhinos are notoriously antisocial.

00:53:27.960 --> 00:53:33.120
Yet here, they come to revel in each other's company.

00:53:33.120 --> 00:53:36.520
This is amazing.

00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:49.880
This is such intimate behavior, which you can only see filming them at night like this.

00:53:49.880 --> 00:53:54.200
It's incredible.

00:53:54.200 --> 00:54:00.360
But it wasn't just cameras that would show a new side to rhinos.

00:54:00.360 --> 00:54:06.160
By concealing tiny radio microphones around the water hole, the crew hoped to eavesdrop

00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:23.080
on the night's activity.

00:54:23.080 --> 00:54:26.080
And what they heard was astonishing.

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:29.080
They're really talkative.

00:54:29.080 --> 00:54:36.960
They really are having a good chat.

00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:43.640
These guys are far more communicative than elephants even.

00:54:43.640 --> 00:54:52.440
They're just going on and on, chatting on.

00:54:52.440 --> 00:54:57.640
It's a beautiful crystal clear night, so we've got beautiful starry shots, loads of amazing

00:54:57.640 --> 00:55:00.640
noise, puffing and howling.

00:55:00.640 --> 00:55:08.600
So it's about two in the morning.

00:55:08.600 --> 00:55:09.600
You've one rhino left out there.

00:55:09.600 --> 00:55:10.600
The rest have gone to bed, but are used to lying down on top of the radio mic.

00:55:10.600 --> 00:55:34.480
The crew prepared for one more night at the water hole under the full moon.

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:45.840
It seems that they're not really here for the water, but more to socialize.

00:55:45.840 --> 00:55:51.440
A bit like going out for the evening.

00:55:51.440 --> 00:55:58.360
He's got some kudu horns on his face, draped over his nose.

00:55:58.360 --> 00:56:05.720
I've got one camera, too.

00:56:05.720 --> 00:56:11.680
These images have a particular poignancy in a world where rhino horn is worth more than

00:56:11.680 --> 00:56:14.640
its weight in gold.

00:56:14.640 --> 00:56:19.320
Poaching is going through a really bad time right now in southern Africa.

00:56:19.320 --> 00:56:27.360
If you averaged it out, a rhino has been killed every day over the last year.

00:56:27.360 --> 00:56:30.400
It's really serious poaching.

00:56:30.400 --> 00:56:39.640
It's a huge concern that what we saw in film just won't happen again, ever.

00:56:39.640 --> 00:56:52.400
It's only now that technology has revealed a new side to the rhino's personality.

00:56:52.400 --> 00:56:58.880
The black rhinoceros is a symbol of the African bush, but it seems that this creature has

00:56:58.880 --> 00:57:05.280
been long misunderstood.

00:57:05.280 --> 00:57:13.840
For the Africa team, revealing giraffes and rhinos in this new light was just the beginning.

00:57:13.840 --> 00:57:41.360
Africa may be a continent we think we know, but it's still full of surprises.
